Passenger pigeons did everything together. They fed on acorns, beechnuts and chestnuts in the great eastern forests, sweeping across the land to wherever the trees had fruited that year.

Their nesting colonies were enormous. One in Wisconsin in 1871 is said to have covered about 2,200 square kilometres, bigger than the city of Delhi. Each pair laid just one egg a year. In such crowds, [[predator|predators]] could only eat a tiny share of the chicks. That strategy worked for thousands of years, until people arrived with new tools.

{{story:telegraph}}

The Atacama looks and behaves so much like Mars that NASA and other space agencies test their [[@mars-rovers|rovers]] there. The soils are dry, salty and full of chemicals similar to those on the red planet. Scientists practise searching for tiny traces of life, which helps them design instruments to send to Mars.

Its clear, dry skies also make it one of the best places on Earth for telescopes. The giant ALMA [[observatory|observatory]] sits on a plateau at about 5,000 m, high above most of the water vapour in the air.

{{story:mars-rovers}}

Inside the cloud, tiny ice crystals rise while heavier soft hail falls. As they knock together, the small crystals tend to pick up positive charge and the hail picks up negative charge. Positive charge gathers at the top of the cloud, and negative charge near the bottom.

When the difference grows big enough, electricity jumps, inside the cloud or down to the ground. The bolt heats the air around it to about 27,000 °C. The air expands so violently that it makes a shock wave, which we hear as thunder.

{{story:lightning-rods}}

DART launched in November 2021 from California and travelled for ten months. It weighed about 570 kg at the end, about as much as a small car. Its camera did all the steering in the final hours, locking on to a target it had never seen.

On 26 September 2022, it slammed into Dimorphos at about 22,000 km/h. The last pictures showed a field of boulders rushing closer. Then the signal stopped. A small Italian satellite called LICIACube, released a couple of weeks earlier, flew past to photograph the blast.

{{story:extinction}}

Your [[@eyes-see|eyes]] have a tiny pit at the centre of the retina called the fovea. It is where your vision is sharpest, and it is what you use to read these words. An eagle has two foveae in each eye. One looks forward, where both eyes overlap. The other looks out to the side, towards the horizon.

In birds of prey, the cells in these spots are crowded together much more densely than in humans. More cells in the same space means finer detail, a bit like a [[@cameras-phones|phone camera]] with more [[pixel|pixels]]. The deep pit may also act like a small [[@lenses-glasses|lens]], stretching the middle of the picture so it looks bigger.

{{story:eyes-see}}

In the 1920s, a Japanese weather scientist named Wasaburo Oishi released more than a thousand small [[@weather-balloons|balloons]] from an [[observatory|observatory]] north-east of [[@tokyo|Tokyo]] and tracked them with a telescope. He found strong winds blowing steadily from the west, high above Japan. He published his results in [[@esperanto|Esperanto]], an invented language, and few scientists elsewhere read them.

The winds became famous during the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]]. American bomber pilots flying high over Japan found themselves battling headwinds so strong that their planes almost stood still. Weather scientists soon named these winds jet streams.

{{story:weather-forecasts}}

Every pattern in a kaleidoscope has the same kind of balance as a snowflake or a flower. Mathematicians call it [[@symmetry|symmetry]]. Fold it along a line through the centre and the two halves match. Turn it by one slice and it looks the same.

With mirrors at 60 degrees, you get six-fold [[symmetry|symmetry]], the same as a snowflake. That's no accident. Water [[molecule|molecules]] join into six-sided crystals, so [[@snowflake-six|snowflakes]] grow six matching arms. [[@rangoli-art|Rangoli]] and kolam artists in India often build designs around a centre in a similar way, with the same pattern repeated in every direction.

{{story:rangoli-symmetry}}

Bowlers rub one side of the ball on their trousers to keep it smooth and shiny, and let the other side get rough. With an older ball, the difference between the two sides helps it swing even when the seam is not doing all the work.

For many years, bowlers used [[@sweat|sweat]] and [[@gross-saliva|saliva]] to help polish the ball. In 2020, because of COVID-19, the ICC stopped players using [[saliva|saliva]], and in 2022 it made the ban permanent. Sweat is still allowed. Anything else, such as sandpaper or bottle tops, counts as ball-tampering and is against the rules.

{{story:airplanes-fly}}

Uruguay met neighbour [[@argentina|Argentina]] in the final. Thousands of Argentine fans crossed the River Plate by boat to watch. The teams argued over the ball, so they played one half with each.

Uruguay won 4–2. The country declared the next day a national holiday. In Buenos Aires, across the river, angry crowds threw stones at the Uruguayan consulate, and the two football associations stopped speaking for a while.

{{story:dogs-domestic}}

This patch is called the visual word form area. It sits low down on the left side of the brain, roughly behind your left ear. Some scientists nickname it the brain's letterbox.

It recognises a word whether it's in CAPITALS or small letters, handwritten or printed, in a newspaper or on a phone. In people who read Hindi, Tamil, English or Chinese, scans show the same area lighting up. Messages from it then travel to areas that handle sound and meaning. Every word you read sets off a quick chain of signals between millions of [[neuron|neurons]].

{{story:alphabet-history}}

By 1879, Thomas Edison and his team in New Jersey had made a light bulb that could glow for many hours. Edison knew a bulb was useless without a supply of electricity, so he designed a whole system: generators, cables, meters, switches and fuses.

On 4 September 1882, his Pearl Street Station in Lower Manhattan began sending power through underground cables to about 80 nearby customers, lighting roughly 400 bulbs. Within a few years, power stations were opening in cities around the world. Edison's system used direct current, which led to a fierce contest with a rival system, a story told in [[@tesla-edison|the war of the currents]].

{{story:light-bulb}}

The trick uses entanglement, a strange link between two particles that [[@einstein|Einstein]] called "spooky action at a distance". Measure one entangled particle and you instantly learn something about its partner, however far apart they are.

Scientists use a pair like this as a bridge. They mix a third particle with one half of the pair, measure them, and send the result to the far end by ordinary radio or laser. The person there uses those results to turn the other half into an exact copy of the third particle's state. The original state is wiped out, so nothing is ever duplicated, and nothing goes faster than light.

{{story:quantum-computers}}

A supercomputer isn't one magic chip. It is thousands of computers, called nodes, packed into rows of cabinets and connected by super-fast cables. Each node may have powerful graphics chips, the same kind used in gaming and AI.

A big problem is chopped into millions of small pieces. Each node works on its own piece and swaps results with its neighbours. It's like a whole school colouring a giant map, each child doing one square, rather than one person colouring all of it. The hard part is making the nodes talk quickly enough that nobody is left waiting.

{{story:big-numbers}}

Tenzing had been on six earlier Everest expeditions. He left sweets and biscuits in the snow as an offering, in the Buddhist tradition. Hillary left a small cross that Hunt had given him. Tenzing unfurled flags of the [[@united-nations|United Nations]], Britain, Nepal and India.

Both men became world-famous. Tenzing later led field training at the new Himalayan Mountaineering Institute in Darjeeling, founded in 1954. Hillary spent decades raising money to build schools and hospitals for Sherpa communities in Nepal.

{{story:bar-headed-goose}}

Genghis Khan reorganised his army into units of 10, 100, 1,000 and 10,000 men. He broke up old tribal groups and mixed men from different tribes, so their loyalty was to the khan, not to their clan.

Leaders were promoted for skill and loyalty rather than noble birth. Scouts and spies gathered information before an attack. A favourite trick was the fake retreat. Riders pretended to flee, drew the enemy into a trap, then turned and surrounded them. When cities resisted, Mongol armies used captured Chinese and Persian engineers to build siege machines.

{{story:mongolia}}

There was no single route and no map. A journey might cover more than 1,000 km (over 600 miles), mostly on foot, mostly at night. People hid by day in barns, attics, cellars and church basements. They waded through streams so dogs could not follow their scent. Many used the [[@north-star|North Star]] to keep heading the right way.

Helpers came from many backgrounds. Free Black communities did much of the work. Quakers, a Christian group that opposed slavery, ran many stations. Each helper usually knew only the next stop, so if one was caught, the whole chain stayed safe. Being caught meant terrible punishment for those escaping, and fines or prison for those helping.

{{story:north-star}}

North of the Arctic Circle, the Sun does strange things. In midsummer it never sets, circling low around the sky at midnight. In midwinter it never rises, and the days are a dim blue twilight or full dark.

This happens because the Earth is tilted. In June the north leans towards the Sun, and in December it leans away, as explained in [[@seasons-why|why we have seasons]]. At the pole itself, there is one sunrise and one sunset a year, six months apart.

{{story:seasons-why}}

A big tree in the Amazon can pull hundreds of litres of water out of the soil in a day and breathe it out through its leaves. Multiply that by hundreds of billions of trees, and the forest releases a vast cloud of water vapour. Scientists call these moving streams of moist air "flying rivers".

The wind carries them west and south, where they fall as rain on farms and cities far away. Some of that water falls on the forest again, so the same water can be recycled many times as it crosses the continent.

{{story:water-cycle}}

Kerala's [[@food-pepper-black-gold|black pepper]], cardamom and cinnamon were famous long before Europeans arrived. Around 2,000 years ago, Roman and Arab traders sailed to ports such as Muziris to buy [[@spices|spices]]. In 1498, the Portuguese sailor Vasco da Gama landed near Kozhikode (Calicut), opening a sea route from Europe. The Portuguese, Dutch and British fought for control of this rich trade.

{{story:spices}}

These [[@trade-routes|trade routes]] brought people too. Christian communities in Kerala trace their faith back many centuries. Arab traders brought Islam, and Kerala has some of India's oldest mosques. In [[@kochi|Kochi]], Jewish families built the Paradesi Synagogue in 1568.

Indian ideas reached Thailand over many centuries through traders, monks and the [[@khmer-empire|Khmer empire]] of Cambodia. The Thai script developed from Khmer writing, which itself grew from the [[@scripts-india|scripts of southern India]].

The Thai version of the Ramayana is called the Ramakien. Its scenes cover the walls of a long gallery around the Temple of the Emerald Buddha in Bangkok. Thailand's kings have taken the name Rama for over two centuries: the current king is Rama X. And the great city of Ayutthaya, capital from 1351 until it was destroyed by a Burmese army in 1767, was named after Ayodhya. Its ruins are now a World Heritage Site.

{{story:ramayana}}

Agatha Christie, an English writer, published her first mystery in 1920. It introduced Hercule Poirot, a fussy Belgian detective with a neat moustache who trusts his "little grey cells". Later she created Miss Marple, an elderly woman in a quiet village who understands people better than any police officer.

Guinness World Records lists Christie as the best-selling fiction writer of all time, with about two billion books sold. Her play The Mousetrap has run in London almost without a break since 1952.

{{story:fingerprints}}

Across India, poets retold the story in their own languages. Kamban wrote a Tamil version around the 1100s. Krittibas Ojha retold it in Bengali in the 1400s. In the 1500s, Tulsidas wrote the *Ramcharitmanas* in Awadhi, a form of [[@hindi-language|Hindi]], and it became one of the most loved books in north India.

Each version differs. In some, Ravana is a pure villain. In others, he is a learned king with a fatal flaw. Jain and Buddhist traditions have their own tellings too. The scholar A. K. Ramanujan wrote a well-known essay called "Three Hundred Ramayanas" about this rich variety.

{{story:diwali-lights}}

The northeast is one of the wettest parts of India, and rice grows well in its valleys and on terraced hill slopes. Many communities grow their own varieties. Manipur's chak-hao is a black rice that turns purple when cooked, and it's used to make a sweet pudding. Assam's joha is a small, fragrant rice, and some sticky rices are steamed inside bamboo tubes.

{{story:bamboo}}

In the hills, rain and cold made it hard to keep fresh food for long. So people learned to dry, smoke and ferment it. Meat hung over the kitchen fire slowly smokes for weeks, and smoked pork is a favourite in Nagaland and other hill states.
